WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.730
Our school got a big table: attendance and

2
00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:06.910
marks for every class, for a whole year.

3
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.520
Our class asked: where do we even start?

4
00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:12.590
We needed a routine.

5
00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.030
Today: a five-question routine for

6
00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:18.810
exploring any table of data.

7
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:21.620
First, what is in it?

8
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:23.580
Count the rows and columns.

9
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.860
What is one row: a pupil,

10
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.080
a class or a day?

11
00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.570
Which columns are numbers, and

12
00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:32.470
which are categories?

13
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.420
Second, the shape.

14
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.640
Draw a histogram of each number column.

15
00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:42.750
Is it a hill, or does it have a long tail?

16
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.160
Third, the centre and the spread.

17
00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.610
Give the median and the range for skewed

18
00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.130
columns, and the mean and the standard

19
00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.930
deviation for symmetric ones.

20
00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.750
Fourth, gaps and oddities.

21
00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:01.790
Look for missing values, outliers and

22
00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:04.890
impossible numbers, like a class with one

23
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.390
hundred and twenty percent attendance.

24
00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.350
Fifth, groups and relationships.

25
00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.270
Compare boys and girls, morning

26
00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:14.810
and afternoon, or villages.

27
00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.080
Draw a scatter plot of

28
00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:18.160
attendance and marks.

29
00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:20.490
Then ask the next question.

30
00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:22.220
Quick check!

31
00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.290
One class shows one hundred and

32
00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:26.810
twenty percent attendance.

33
00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.110
Which step of the routine catches this?

34
00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:32.890
Gaps and oddities.

35
00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:35.570
Attendance can't pass one hundred

36
00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.810
percent, so something went wrong,

37
00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.110
maybe pupils counted twice.

38
00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:43.510
Fix or flag it before you compare classes.

39
00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.190
Then write three findings,

40
00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:48.510
each with evidence.

41
00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:52.150
For example: attendance is lowest

42
00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:54.370
in the rainy months, with a median

43
00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:56.150
of eighty percent in July.

44
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.170
A finding needs a number and

45
00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:00.030
where it came from.

46
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:01.800
Your turn.

47
00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.870
Which is the better finding: attendance

48
00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:08.370
is bad, or attendance falls to a median

49
00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:09.810
of eighty percent in July?

50
00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.960
The second one: it has a

51
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:14.120
number and a time.

52
00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.130
Remember the routine: what is in it,

53
00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:20.590
shape, centre and spread, gaps and

54
00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:23.010
oddities, groups and relationships.

55
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.080
Then write findings with evidence.

56
00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:29.430
Try it yourself: pick a small

57
00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.190
table, like a week of weather, and

58
00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.470
go through the five questions.

59
00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:35.970
Write three findings with

60
00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.290
numbers, and one next question.
